-- title:
--   The unit-ball volume constant is finite
-- statement:
--   Let $\omega_d := \texttt{unitBallVol } d$ denote the Lebesgue measure of the unit ball of
--   $\mathbb{R}^d$, regarded as an element of the extended non-negative reals $[0,\infty]$. Then
--
--   $$\omega_d \neq \infty .$$
--
--   The constant $\omega_d$ appears as a normalising factor whenever a cube is compared with a ball —
--   for instance in the Vitali-family estimate that a cube of side $h$ containing a point occupies a
--   fixed fraction of the volume of the circumscribed ball. Its finiteness is the side condition needed
--   to cancel or divide by it in `ℝ≥0∞` arithmetic, where such cancellations are illegal at $\infty$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The statement is phrased in `ℝ≥0∞`, where the volume of a bounded set is a
--   priori only known to be an element of $[0,\infty]$; recording $\omega_d \ne \top$ separately is what
--   allows the multiplicative manipulations that follow.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/LebesgueDiff.lean#L94-L94

